// GTY website — interim content database.
//
// ⚠️ THIS DATABASE CONTRADICTS `Documents/guruji-travel-yatra-website-CLAUDE.md`
// §2 (line 56): "Do not build a parallel database for leads or tour content",
// and §10, which makes this site a pure read-client of the ERP's content API.
// BLOCKERS.md §1b further records that CLAUDE.md §6 "explicitly forbids working
// around it with a duplicate content model".
//
// The user has knowingly and explicitly OVERRIDDEN that constraint — see
// `documentation/technical-specification/BLOCKERS.md` §5 ("Interim content
// database"). This schema is an INTERIM measure while the CRM finishes its own
// endpoints. It holds CONTENT ONLY. It holds NO LEADS: lead submission remains
// log-only (BLOCKERS.md §6) and CLAUDE.md's lead prohibition is NOT overridden.
//
// ⚠️ AMENDED 2026-09-17 — the "NO ADMIN UI" half of §5's override is GONE.
// §5 scoped its override with "NO ADMIN SURFACE. No `/admin`, no login, no CMS",
// and this header used to repeat that. The user PERMANENTLY REVERSED that rule on
// 2026-09-17 (see `memory/project_gty_admin_reversal.md`): a staff admin CMS is
// now built into this same app. `AdminUser` below is the first model that serves
// it, and it is the ONLY non-content model here. The lead prohibition is
// untouched and still holds — if a lead/customer model ever appears in this file,
// it did NOT come from the admin reversal.
//
// When the ERP's content-read API lands, the migration path is the one the
// architecture was built for: a new `ErpContentAdapter implements ContentPort`
// replaces `PrismaContentAdapter` at the composition root
// (`src/content/content-adapter.ts`), and this whole directory is deleted. No
// page or component changes either way — that is the entire point of the port.

// ## `relationJoins` (preview) — enabled 2026-10-10, BLOCKERS.md item 16
//
// Without it, Prisma 5 resolves every `include` level as a SEPARATE round trip.
// The database is remote (Railway, ~240ms per round trip from here), so
// `getPackageRails()`'s rail → items → tour → destination/gallery include was 5
// sequential queries and ~1.2s, and `/tour-packages` 503'd under prefetch load.
// With it, the default load strategy becomes JOIN (one LATERAL/JSON query per
// call): measured 5 queries/~1210ms → 1 query/~300ms on that call.
//
// This changes the strategy for EVERY `include` in the app, not just the rails.
// Verified before enabling, against the live DB: every include-shaped query in
// `src/` (public catalogue, popular, departures, admin list, admin detail)
// returned byte-identical results under both strategies — except the ORDER of
// rows tied on `updatedAt` in the admin list, which Postgres never guaranteed;
// that query now has an explicit `slug` tiebreaker. Server is PostgreSQL 18.
// To opt a single query back out: `relationLoadStrategy: 'query'`.
generator client {
  provider        = "prisma-client-js"
  previewFeatures = ["relationJoins"]
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
  url      = env("DATABASE_URL")
}

/// A STAFF member who can sign in to `/admin`. Phase A of the admin CMS.
///
/// NOT a visitor account and never will be. The public site has no signup, no
/// password reset and no self-service anything — `/admin/login` is the only auth
/// surface, rows are created ONLY by `prisma/seed-admin.ts`, and the privacy
/// policy says so in as many words. Adding a visitor-facing registration flow
/// would be a different product decision, not an extension of this one.
///
/// ## No `role` column, deliberately
///
/// Every row is a full admin. A `role` enum with one member is a fiction that
/// invites `if (user.role === 'ADMIN')` checks which are tautologies today and
/// silently wrong the day a second role appears. When GTY actually needs
/// tiered access, that is a schema change WITH the authorization code that
/// enforces it — `memory/feedback_qg_unenforced-safety-flag.md` records four
/// separate times a column shipped here without the enforcement behind it.
///
/// ## No sessions table, deliberately
///
/// Sessions are stateless JWTs in an httpOnly cookie (`src/server/auth/auth.ts`).
/// A `Session`/`Account`/`VerificationToken` triple is what NextAuth's Prisma
/// ADAPTER needs; the Credentials provider does not use an adapter at all, so
/// those tables would be created empty and stay empty. The cost of this choice
/// is stated plainly in that file: a JWT cannot be revoked server-side before it
/// expires.
model AdminUser {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  /// Lowercased at every write and every lookup (`normalizeAdminEmail()` in
  /// `src/server/auth/admin-user.ts`). Postgres `@unique` is case-SENSITIVE, so
  /// without that normalisation `Owner@gty.com` and `owner@gty.com` would be two
  /// distinct rows that both satisfy the constraint — i.e. the uniqueness this
  /// column advertises would not be the uniqueness anyone relies on.
  email String @unique

  /// bcrypt hash, cost 12 — `$2b$` format, 60 chars. NEVER a plaintext password
  /// and never a reversible encryption. Written only by
  /// `hashPassword()` (`src/server/auth/password.ts`), which is the single
  /// place that decides the cost factor.
  ///
  /// This column is excluded from every query that can reach a React component:
  /// `findAdminUserForLogin()` is the ONLY reader, it lives behind
  /// `import 'server-only'`, and it returns the hash to the credentials
  /// `authorize()` callback and nowhere else.
  passwordHash String

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// NULL until the first successful sign-in. Written by the `signIn` event, not
  /// by `authorize()` — a correct password that then fails a later check must
  /// not be recorded as a login. Best-effort: a write failure here is logged and
  /// swallowed, because losing a timestamp must never cost a valid user their
  /// session.
  lastLoginAt DateTime?
}

/// Mirrors the `DestinationCategory` union in `src/content/types.ts` EXACTLY.
///
/// A Prisma enum rather than a bare `String` so a typo cannot reach the
/// category-chip derivation (`get-category-chips.ts`), which builds the public
/// filter UI straight off these values. `Honeymoon` and `Wildlife` are absent on
/// purpose (BLOCKERS.md Decision C) — do not add them back here without
/// re-reading that decision.
///
/// `FamilyTrip` is the only member whose Prisma name differs from the domain
/// string, because Prisma enum members cannot contain a space. `@map` keeps the
/// stored value byte-identical to the domain string; `prisma-enums.ts` owns the
/// name↔string conversion and a test asserts the mapping covers the union.
enum DestinationCategory {
  Spiritual
  Mountains
  Cultural
  Family
  Adventure
  International
  Beach
  Himalaya
  FamilyTrip @map("Family Trip")
  Pilgrimage
}

/// Publication state of a `Tour` — Phase B of the admin CMS.
///
/// TWO MEMBERS, AND NO `ARCHIVED` MEMBER. Archiving is `Tour.archivedAt`, a
/// nullable timestamp, not a third enum value. The two facts are independent:
/// the business needs to know whether an archived tour *was* live when it was
/// pulled, and folding that into one column would destroy it. It also means a
/// tour cannot be simultaneously "archived" and "draft", which it genuinely can
/// be. `BrochureStatus` above makes the opposite choice for a good reason — a
/// brochure's `PENDING_UPLOAD` and `ARCHIVED` really are mutually exclusive
/// lifecycle stages — so the two enums are not inconsistent, they model
/// different things.
///
/// **`DRAFT` is the default and that is load-bearing, not a convenience.** A row
/// inserted by any path that forgets to set this column — a future import
/// script, a `createMany`, a psql session — is invisible to the public site
/// rather than accidentally live. The safe direction is the default direction.
///
/// ENFORCEMENT, not just a column: `isPubliclyListable()`
/// (`src/content/public-listing.ts`) requires `status === 'PUBLISHED'`, and
/// EVERY public query on both adapters ends in that predicate. See the long note
/// on `Tour.status` below, and `memory/feedback_qg_unenforced-safety-flag.md`
/// for the five occasions a column shipped here without the behaviour behind it.
enum TourStatus {
  DRAFT
  PUBLISHED
}

/// WHO a tour is sold to — a SECOND, INDEPENDENT axis from `DestinationCategory`.
///
/// ## Why this is not folded into `DestinationCategory`
///
/// `DestinationCategory` (Adventure/Spiritual/Family/…) is the THEMATIC axis, and
/// it is load-bearing public UI: `get-category-chips.ts` derives the
/// `/tour-packages` filter chips straight off it, and `/api/tours?category=`
/// validates against it. Adding `Domestic`/`Group Tour` to that enum would put
/// four new chips on the public filter bar that answer a different question from
/// the ones beside them, and would make `category` mean two things at once — so
/// a tour could no longer be both "Spiritual" and "Domestic", which is the normal
/// case, not the exception.
///
/// Note `International` appears in BOTH enums. That is not duplication to clean
/// up: the `DestinationCategory` member describes a destination's character,
/// this one describes the audience the tour is sold to, and a tour can carry one
/// without the other. They are separate Postgres types and nothing converts
/// between them.
///
/// Stored as a Postgres ARRAY on `Tour` rather than a join table (see
/// `Tour.audienceTags`).
enum TourAudienceTag {
  Domestic
  International
  GroupTour      @map("Group Tour")
  CustomEligible @map("Custom-eligible")
}

/// Lifecycle of a brochure record — product-spec §7 ("Brochure Library").
///
/// `ARCHIVED` exists because the product spec is explicit: "Never hard-delete a
/// brochure tied to existing leads/bookings — archive instead."
///
/// `PENDING_UPLOAD` is the state EVERY seeded brochure is in, and it is not a
/// placeholder for tidiness: no real brochure PDF exists anywhere in this
/// project, so `pdfUrl` is NULL on every seeded row. A brochure may only move to
/// `PUBLISHED` when a real file backs it.
enum BrochureStatus {
  PENDING_UPLOAD
  PUBLISHED
  ARCHIVED
}

/// A place tours are sold to. One table serves both uses in `src/content`:
/// the FK target for `Tour`/`GroupDeparture`, and the International
/// Destinations rail (`isInternational` + `internationalOrder`).
///
/// `region`/`category`/`tagline` are nullable because the design export renders
/// only a name and a photo for the international cards — `types.ts` marks them
/// optional rather than inventing them, and the DB must not invent them either.
model Destination {
  /// Kebab-case, produced by `slugify()` — never hand-typed (packages.fixture.ts).
  slug        String               @id
  name        String               @unique
  region      String?
  category    DestinationCategory?
  tagline     String?

  /// OPAQUE STRING KEY, NOT A URL — Assumption A4. No real photo files exist in
  /// this project; the column holds the destination's display name and the card
  /// components render a token-coloured `PhotoPlaceholder` from it. Storing a
  /// plausible-looking CDN URL here would fabricate an asset that does not
  /// exist. Renamed from the domain field's `photoUrl` precisely so nothing
  /// downstream mistakes it for a fetchable address.
  photoKey    String
  photosCount Int?

  /// True for the 5 destinations on Home's International rail
  /// (`international.fixture.ts`). Malaysia is deliberately NOT seeded: the
  /// export lists it but no tour backs it (BLOCKERS.md Decision C).
  isInternational    Boolean @default(false)
  /// Display order within the International rail. NULL when not international.
  internationalOrder Int?

  tours      Tour[]
  departures GroupDeparture[]

  @@index([isInternational, internationalOrder])
}

/// A sellable tour — the `Package` type in `src/content/types.ts`.
/// SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for tour data, exactly as `packages.fixture.ts` is
/// today. Rail membership and Home's "Popular" selection are VIEWS over this
/// table (`RailTour`, `popularOrder`), never second copies of a tour.
model Tour {
  slug            String              @id
  name            String              @unique
  destinationSlug String
  destination     Destination         @relation(fields: [destinationSlug], references: [slug])
  category        DestinationCategory

  /// e.g. "9 Days 8 Nights" — the catalogue's own duration string.
  durationLabel String
  /// e.g. "Shimla - Kaza - Chandratal".
  subtitle      String
  /// Opaque string key, not a URL — see `Destination.photoKey`.
  ///
  /// STILL THE FALLBACK, not dead weight. `coverImageUrl` below supersedes it
  /// when a real image has been uploaded; a tour with no upload keeps rendering
  /// the token-coloured `PhotoPlaceholder` built from this key, which is what
  /// all 22 seeded tours still do. The two are read in that order by
  /// `PackageCard`/`PhotoPlaceholder` and nowhere else.
  photoKey      String
  /// The design export's INHERITED figure, and only a fallback. The "N Photos"
  /// badge is derived at READ time by `PrismaContentAdapter` (`toPackage`):
  /// `galleryImages.length` when the tour has uploaded photos, this column when
  /// it has none. The admin write path does NOT keep this in step with the
  /// gallery — `createTour` writes 0 and `updateTour`/`toColumnData`
  /// deliberately never write it. (Corrected 2026-09-19, M5: this note used to
  /// describe a save-time derivation that was reverted in Phase C after it
  /// silently changed a seeded tour's count, 11 → 0, on an unrelated edit.)
  photosCount   Int

  /// Pre-formatted for display, e.g. "₹42,500". Kept as the display string
  /// rather than an integer + currency: every consumer renders it verbatim, and
  /// re-deriving "₹42,500" from 42500 would invent a formatting decision the
  /// design export already made. `getPriceRange()` parses it when it needs a
  /// number.
  priceFrom     String
  priceWas      String?
  savingsLabel  String?
  seatsLabel    String?
  isHot         Boolean @default(false)

  /// SAFETY FLAG — carried across from `Package.pricingUnverified` as a real
  /// column, and ENFORCED, not merely stored.
  ///
  /// The design export transcribed 3 tours twice with disagreeing price/duration
  /// values; neither transcription is confirmed, so these tours may not quote a
  /// price or a "was ₹X / Save ₹Y" badge publicly.
  ///
  /// Enforcement lives in `PrismaContentAdapter`, which routes every public
  /// query through the SAME `isPubliclyListable()` predicate the fixture path
  /// uses (`src/content/public-listing.ts`) — so a flagged row cannot reach any
  /// listing, and `/tour-packages/[slug]` 404s for it, identically to today.
  /// See `memory/feedback_qg_unenforced-safety-flag.md`: a flag that only exists
  /// as a column is the exact defect that memory exists to prevent.
  pricingUnverified Boolean @default(false)

  /// SAFETY GATE — the draft/published boundary. Phase B of the admin CMS.
  ///
  /// `DRAFT` by default, so a half-written tour is never accidentally live. A
  /// draft is invisible to EVERY public surface: `/api/tours`,
  /// `/api/tours/[slug]` (404), Home's rails, `/tour-packages`, and
  /// `generateStaticParams()`.
  ///
  /// This column is enforced in exactly ONE place, deliberately:
  /// `isPubliclyListable()` in `src/content/public-listing.ts`, which every
  /// public query on BOTH adapters ends in. The `where: { status: 'PUBLISHED' }`
  /// clauses in `PrismaContentAdapter` are an OPTIMISATION — they keep unwanted
  /// rows off the wire — and the predicate is the gate. If the two ever
  /// disagree, the predicate wins, which is the safe direction, and
  /// `prisma-content-adapter.test.ts` proves it by returning a draft row from a
  /// mock that IGNORES the where clause and asserting the adapter still
  /// withholds it.
  ///
  /// `memory/feedback_qg_unenforced-safety-flag.md` records five occasions in
  /// this project where a safety flag was added as a column, documented, and
  /// never read by anything. This is the sixth opportunity and the most
  /// expensive one: an unenforced `status` publishes unfinished content rather
  /// than merely quoting a doubtful price.
  status TourStatus @default(DRAFT)

  /// The tour's cover photo — Phase C, and the FIRST real asset URL in this
  /// project. Every other image column (`photoKey`, `Destination.photoKey`,
  /// `Brochure.coverImageKey`) is an opaque display key precisely because no
  /// real files existed; this one holds an https Cloudinary `secure_url` and is
  /// rendered as an actual `<img>`.
  ///
  /// NULLABLE, and NULL on all 22 existing tours — nobody has uploaded a cover
  /// for them, and backfilling a plausible CDN path would fabricate an asset.
  /// The migration adds no default for the same reason.
  ///
  /// REQUIRED TO PUBLISH, though, which is the change that makes this column
  /// something other than an inert field: `publishableTourSchema` refuses a
  /// publish without it (the user's original spec: "Cover image (single,
  /// required)"), and `PackageCard` + the detail page render it in place of the
  /// placeholder. Phase B could not require it because uploads did not exist —
  /// see BLOCKERS.md item 11 for that reversal.
  coverImageUrl String?

  /// Cloudinary's `public_id` for `coverImageUrl`, stored beside it because the
  /// URL is not reliably parseable back into one (folders, versions, format
  /// suffixes and transformation segments all sit in the same path).
  ///
  /// Needed for any future delete/transform call. Nothing deletes assets today —
  /// replacing a cover uploads a NEW asset and repoints this pair, deliberately
  /// leaving the old one in Cloudinary so the live URL never breaks mid-swap.
  /// Two scalar columns rather than a child table because they are one asset
  /// written in one statement; they cannot drift the way two parallel ARRAYS
  /// would, which is why the gallery below is a table instead.
  coverImagePublicId String?

  /// Set when a tour is ARCHIVED; NULL for every live tour.
  ///
  /// A timestamp rather than a boolean or a `TourStatus.ARCHIVED` member,
  /// because "when" is the part the business will ask about, and a boolean
  /// throws it away. Independent of `status` on purpose — an archived tour
  /// keeps the `status` it had when it was pulled, so the record still says
  /// whether it was ever live.
  ///
  /// ARCHIVING IS NOT DELETION. Nothing in the admin UI deletes a `Tour` row:
  /// product-spec §7's brochure rule ("never hard-delete something tied to
  /// existing leads/bookings — archive instead") is the same principle, and a
  /// tour is the thing leads are *about*. An archived tour is withheld from
  /// every public surface by the SAME `isPubliclyListable()` predicate that
  /// gates `status`, and is hidden from the DEFAULT admin list but remains
  /// findable there under the "Archived" / "All" status filter.
  archivedAt DateTime?

  /// WHO this tour is sold to — Domestic / International / Group Tour /
  /// Custom-eligible. See `TourAudienceTag` for why this is a separate axis from
  /// `category` and must not be folded into it.
  ///
  /// A Postgres ARRAY rather than a join table: the membership carries no data
  /// of its own (no order, no per-tag note, no timestamp), so a join table would
  /// be three extra files and a join to store what a column already stores.
  /// `RailTour` above is a join table precisely because rail membership DOES
  /// carry data — `sortOrder`. That is the test applied here, not a blanket
  /// preference.
  ///
  /// Empty by default and legitimately empty: these tags are new in Phase B, so
  /// every existing tour has none until someone categorises it. Nothing public
  /// reads them yet — they are admin-side categorisation, and a public filter
  /// built on them is a later, explicit decision.
  audienceTags TourAudienceTag[]

  /// Card-length description. NULLABLE and NULL on every existing tour.
  ///
  /// NOT a rename of `subtitle`, which holds the ROUTE LINE ("Shimla - Kaza -
  /// Chandratal") and is rendered as such by `PackageCard` and the detail page.
  /// The two were checked against each other before this column was added: no
  /// existing field holds prose. Introducing this as a nullable column rather
  /// than backfilling `subtitle` into it avoids inventing a description for 22
  /// tours nobody has written one for.
  shortDescription String?

  /// Full prose for the detail page. Nullable for the same reason as
  /// `shortDescription` — no tour in this project has one yet, and the detail
  /// page renders only what exists (BLOCKERS.md Decision D).
  fullDescription String?

  /// e.g. "March to June". NULLABLE, and per the product spec this is for
  /// REGULAR tours only — a fixed-departure group tour's answer is its departure
  /// date, so the admin form hides this field when `Group Tour` is among the
  /// `audienceTags`. NULL means "not stated", never "any time of year".
  bestTimeToVisit String?

  /// What the price covers / does not cover. Two Postgres string ARRAYS.
  ///
  /// Arrays and not child tables: an inclusion is a bare line of text with no
  /// per-item fields, no ordering beyond its position, and nothing referencing
  /// it. `TourItineraryDay` and `TourFaq` below ARE child tables because their
  /// items carry two or three fields each. Same test as `audienceTags`.
  inclusions String[]
  exclusions String[]

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// Drives the admin list's "Last updated" column. Added in Phase B because
  /// until there was an admin UI nothing could edit a tour, so there was no
  /// update to stamp.
  ///
  /// `@default(now())` as well as `@updatedAt`: `@updatedAt` is applied by the
  /// Prisma CLIENT on write and emits no database default, so without this the
  /// column could not be added to a table that already held 22 rows. The
  /// backfilled value is the MIGRATION's timestamp, which is not when those
  /// tours were really authored — see the note in the migration SQL. It is an
  /// upper bound, not a fact, and the first real edit replaces it.
  updatedAt DateTime @default(now()) @updatedAt

  /// Global order of `ContentPort.getAllTours()` — rail order first, then the
  /// two popular-only tours, matching `packages.fixture.ts`'s construction
  /// order. Explicit because SQL has no inherent row order and the catalogue's
  /// sequence is an authored design decision.
  catalogueOrder Int @unique

  /// Position in Home's "Popular Destinations" grid, in the export's own order.
  /// NULL for tours Home does not feature. A nullable ordering column rather
  /// than a join table: the selection carries no data of its own beyond order.
  popularOrder Int? @unique

  railLinks RailTour[]
  brochure  Brochure?

  itineraryDays TourItineraryDay[]
  faqs          TourFaq[]
  pricingTiers  TourPricingTier[]
  galleryImages TourGalleryImage[]
  departures    GroupDeparture[]

  @@index([destinationSlug])
  @@index([pricingUnverified])
  /// The public listing gate's own index. Every public tour query filters on
  /// exactly this pair, so it is the pair worth indexing together.
  @@index([status, archivedAt])
}

/// One day of a tour's itinerary — Phase B.
///
/// A child table rather than a JSON column on `Tour`: each day carries three
/// fields and an order, the admin form edits them as rows, and a JSON blob would
/// put validation of that structure entirely in application code with the
/// database unable to say anything about it.
///
/// `onDelete: Cascade` — but note nothing in the admin UI deletes a `Tour`
/// (archiving is a timestamp, see `Tour.archivedAt`), so this cascade is a
/// correctness backstop for the seed's `deleteMany()` reset and for any future
/// genuine deletion, NOT the archive path. Archiving a tour leaves every
/// itinerary day intact, which is the point of archiving.
model TourItineraryDay {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())
  tourSlug String
  tour     Tour   @relation(fields: [tourSlug], references: [slug], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// 1-based, and the ORDER as well as the label ("Day 3"). A separate
  /// `sortOrder` would be a second source of truth for the same fact and could
  /// disagree with it.
  dayNumber   Int
  title       String
  description String

  /// Days are renumbered contiguously from 1 on every save, so this holds.
  @@unique([tourSlug, dayNumber])
  @@index([tourSlug])
}

/// One uploaded gallery photo — Phase C.
///
/// ## Why a child table and not `Tour.galleryImageUrls String[]`
///
/// An array of URLs was the obvious shape and is what the task sketched, but a
/// gallery image is not a bare string: it carries a Cloudinary `publicId` (which
/// the URL cannot be reliably parsed back into) and its intrinsic dimensions,
/// which `next/image`-style rendering wants so the slot does not reflow.
///
/// Storing those as SIBLING ARRAYS — `galleryImageUrls[]` + `galleryPublicIds[]`
/// — is the shape to avoid: nothing in the database ties index 2 of one to index
/// 2 of the other, so a partial write leaves a URL pointing at one asset and a
/// publicId naming another, and the database cannot tell you it happened. That
/// is `memory/feedback_qg_unenforced-safety-flag.md`'s variant-4 defect (one
/// real-world entity split across two stores that drift) reproduced inside a
/// single row.
///
/// So this follows the test `Tour.inclusions` and `TourFaq` already state: bare
/// lines of text are arrays, items with fields of their own are tables. This has
/// three fields and an order, exactly like `TourFaq`.
///
/// `sortOrder` is 0-based and rewritten from array position on every save, the
/// same discipline `childCreates()` applies to itinerary days and FAQs, so
/// removing a middle image cannot leave a gap that violates the unique
/// constraint. Reordering in the admin UI is therefore just reordering the array
/// and saving.
model TourGalleryImage {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())
  tourSlug String
  tour     Tour   @relation(fields: [tourSlug], references: [slug], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Cloudinary `secure_url` — a real https address, unlike `Tour.photoKey`.
  url      String
  /// Cloudinary `public_id`. See `Tour.coverImagePublicId` for why it is stored
  /// rather than derived from the URL.
  publicId String

  /// Intrinsic pixel dimensions as Cloudinary reported them at upload. Nullable
  /// because they are whatever the upload response contained — not invented if
  /// absent.
  width  Int?
  height Int?

  sortOrder Int

  @@unique([tourSlug, sortOrder])
  @@index([tourSlug])
}

/// One question/answer on a tour's detail page — Phase B.
model TourFaq {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())
  tourSlug String
  tour     Tour   @relation(fields: [tourSlug], references: [slug], onDelete: Cascade)

  question String
  answer   String

  /// 0-based display order. Unlike `TourItineraryDay.dayNumber` this is NOT part
  /// of the visible content — an FAQ has no "FAQ 3" label — so it is a plain
  /// ordering column.
  sortOrder Int

  @@unique([tourSlug, sortOrder])
  @@index([tourSlug])
}

/// One named price tier — "Standard" / "Deluxe" / "Premium" — Phase B.
///
/// ## Tiers are a PER-TOUR CHOICE, and the choice is the row count
///
/// `memory/project_gty_admin_reversal.md` records the user's clarification: some
/// tours have one price, others have several named tiers, and the admin decides
/// per tour. That choice is represented by THE PRESENCE OR ABSENCE OF ROWS HERE
/// and by nothing else:
///
///   - zero rows  → the tour uses its own `priceFrom`/`priceWas` (unchanged);
///   - 1+ rows    → the tour shows the tiers.
///
/// There is deliberately NO `Tour.usesPricingTiers` boolean. A flag plus the
/// rows is two sources of truth for one fact, and they drift: a flag set true
/// with the last tier deleted renders a tour with no price at all, and a flag
/// set false with rows present hides pricing the admin entered and can still
/// see in the form. Deriving it cannot drift, because there is nothing to drift
/// from. This is the same reasoning `sold-out.ts` applies to `isSoldOut`
/// (derived from `seatsLabel`, never stored).
model TourPricingTier {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())
  tourSlug String
  tour     Tour   @relation(fields: [tourSlug], references: [slug], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// e.g. "Standard", "Deluxe", "Premium". Free text, not an enum: GTY names
  /// these per tour and an enum would be this file guessing their commercial
  /// vocabulary.
  name String

  /// Pre-formatted display string, e.g. "₹42,500" — the SAME contract as
  /// `Tour.priceFrom`, which exists for the reason given there (every consumer
  /// renders it verbatim). A tier priced as an integer here and a string there
  /// would mean two formatting rules for one concept.
  price String

  /// What this tier includes over the one below it. Nullable: a tier may be
  /// priced without a written justification.
  description String?

  sortOrder Int

  @@unique([tourSlug, sortOrder])
  @@index([tourSlug])
}

/// One of the 4 `/tour-packages` rails — `PackageRail` in `types.ts`.
model Rail {
  slug      String     @id
  title     String     @unique
  sortOrder Int        @unique
  items     RailTour[]
}

/// Rail membership. A join table because a rail's contents are ordered and a
/// tour's rail is not a property of the tour.
model RailTour {
  railSlug  String
  tourSlug  String
  sortOrder Int
  rail      Rail   @relation(fields: [railSlug], references: [slug], onDelete: Cascade)
  tour      Tour   @relation(fields: [tourSlug], references: [slug], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@id([railSlug, tourSlug])
  @@unique([railSlug, sortOrder])
  @@index([tourSlug])
}

/// A fixed-departure group tour — `GroupDeparture` in `src/content/types.ts`.
///
/// DELIBERATELY NOT a variant of `Tour`, mirroring the domain model: a departure
/// carries no `priceWas`/`savingsLabel`/`photosCount`, and inheriting them would
/// invite a "Save ₹X" badge this data cannot support. The two overlap by subject,
/// not by contract. `dateRangeLabel`, `seatsLabel`, `boardingPoints` and
/// `tourManagerLabel` exist in NO other source.
model GroupDeparture {
  slug            String              @id
  /// The card title. NOT unique (2026-09-25, migration
  /// `20260925100000_departure_name_not_unique`): two batches of the same tour
  /// are two departures with the same title and different dates, which is the
  /// ordinary case the admin batch editor exists for. `slug` is the identity;
  /// leads carry the batch's `dateRangeLabel` beside the name, so a lead is
  /// still attributable to one batch.
  name            String
  destinationSlug String
  destination     Destination         @relation(fields: [destinationSlug], references: [slug])
  category        DestinationCategory
  /// Opaque string key, not a URL — see `Destination.photoKey`.
  photoKey        String

  /// The catalogue tour this departure is a dated batch OF — NEW in Phase B, and
  /// NULLABLE on purpose.
  ///
  /// Before Phase B this model was destination-scoped only: `prisma/seed.ts`
  /// derived `destinationSlug` from the departure's display name and there was
  /// no link to `Tour` at all. That made "manage this tour's batch dates and
  /// seats" unanswerable, which is what the admin form needs — hence this
  /// column rather than a duplicate batch-date model on `Tour` (the task's
  /// explicit instruction, and correct: `dateRangeLabel`/`seatsLabel`/
  /// `boardingPoints`/`tourManagerLabel` exist here and nowhere else, so a
  /// second model would be a second transcription of the same real-world thing —
  /// exactly the drift `memory/feedback_qg_unenforced-safety-flag.md` variant 4
  /// is about).
  ///
  /// NULLABLE because the link is genuinely partial and must not be faked. A
  /// departure's name carries a suffix the catalogue's does not ("Do Dham Yatra
  /// — Kedarnath & Badrinath" vs "Do Dham Yatra"), and not every departure has a
  /// catalogue twin at all. `prisma/seed.ts` links only the pairs it can match
  /// exactly and leaves the rest NULL; inventing a link for the others would
  /// attach real seat counts and dates to the wrong tour.
  ///
  /// A NULL here does NOT hide the departure — an unlinked departure answers to no
  /// tour's visibility and is listed as-is.
  ///
  /// A NON-NULL link DOES gate it (2026-09-25): `getGroupDepartures()` ends in
  /// `toPublicDeparture()` (`src/content/public-listing.ts`), which withholds the
  /// departure while its tour is a DRAFT or ARCHIVED. Before that, archiving a
  /// tour left its departures listed and bookable on `/group-tours` and
  /// `GET /api/group-tours`. A `pricingUnverified` tour's departure is NOT
  /// withheld — it is marked `priceUnverified` at read time (BLOCKERS.md item 9).
  ///
  /// Written by the seed (derived, `src/content/departure-tour-link.ts`) and by
  /// the admin tour form, which creates batches already linked to their tour.
  /// The live database predates this column's seeding — all 4 rows were NULL
  /// until migration `20260925090000_link_seeded_departures_to_tours` wrote the
  /// two links the rule derives. Without it the gate covered no real row.
  tourSlug String?
  tour     Tour?   @relation(fields: [tourSlug], references: [slug], onDelete: SetNull)

  /// e.g. "12 — 18 May 2026", verbatim from the export.
  dateRangeLabel   String
  /// Month key the chip filter groups by, e.g. "May".
  month            String
  /// The departure's OWN duration string, e.g. "6N / 7D".
  durationLabel    String
  boardingPoints   String
  tourManagerLabel String
  priceFrom        String
  /// e.g. "14 seats left" | "Sold out". The export encodes sold-out state ONLY
  /// as this literal string.
  seatsLabel       String
  /// Caveat-script accent overlaid on the photo; contains newlines.
  scriptAccent     String

  /// SAFETY FLAG — `GroupDeparture.durationUnverified`. True when this
  /// departure's duration contradicts the same tour's duration in the
  /// catalogue. Surfaced with a visible marker rather than withheld (a
  /// departure's date and seat count are real regardless).
  durationUnverified Boolean @default(false)

  /// SAFETY FLAG — `GroupDeparture.priceUnverified`. True when this price is the
  /// SAME unconfirmed figure the catalogue withholds for the same tour
  /// (BLOCKERS.md item 9). A SECOND flag rather than a read-through to
  /// `Tour.pricingUnverified` because a departure is not a `Tour` — and the seed
  /// asserts the two stay in sync, in both directions, so confirming a price
  /// cannot clear one flag and leave the other.
  priceUnverified Boolean @default(false)

  /// Display order of the departures list.
  sortOrder Int @unique

  @@index([destinationSlug])
  @@index([month])
  @@index([tourSlug])
}

/// Per-tour brochure metadata — product-spec §7 ("Brochure Library").
///
/// NEW in this pass: no brochure fixture exists today. Field list is shaped to
/// the ERP's eventual Brochure Library (version, upload date, file size, status,
/// download count, last updated + Preview/Replace/Download/Archive actions) so
/// the data can migrate rather than be re-modelled.
///
/// NOTHING HERE FABRICATES AN ASSET. Every seeded row has `pdfUrl = NULL`,
/// `status = PENDING_UPLOAD`, `fileSizeBytes = NULL`, `uploadedAt = NULL` and
/// `downloadCount = 0`, because no brochure PDF exists in this project. A
/// plausible-looking URL here would be the same class of defect as the invented
/// Instagram handle and the "▶ Watch Full Video" button with no video behind it
/// (`memory/feedback_qg_export-verbatim-false-copy.md`).
model Brochure {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())
  /// One current brochure per tour. Version HISTORY is deliberately not modelled
  /// yet — `version` records which revision this row is; a `BrochureVersion`
  /// table is ERP-side work and nobody has asked for it here.
  tourSlug String @unique
  tour     Tour   @relation(fields: [tourSlug], references: [slug], onDelete: Cascade)

  title String
  /// Derived from the tour's real `subtitle` (e.g. "Shimla - Kaza - Chandratal"),
  /// which IS the itinerary in the only form this project has it. No prose
  /// itinerary has been written for any tour, so none is invented here.
  itinerarySummary String
  /// NULLABLE AND NULL for every `pricingUnverified` tour. The catalogue
  /// withholds those prices publicly; a brochure record republishing the same
  /// unconfirmed figure would reopen exactly the hole BLOCKERS.md item 9 closed.
  priceLabel       String?
  /// NULL on every seeded row. Tours carry NO date data anywhere in this project
  /// — only `GroupDeparture` has real dates, and inferring a tour's brochure
  /// validity window from a departure would be an invention. Populated only when
  /// the business supplies real dates.
  dateRangeLabel   String?
  /// Opaque string key, not a URL — see `Destination.photoKey`.
  coverImageKey    String
  category         DestinationCategory

  /// NULL until a real PDF is uploaded. NEVER a synthesised path such as
  /// `/brochures/spiti-valley.pdf` — a link that 404s is worse than an absent
  /// one, and `status` already carries the "not available yet" meaning.
  ///
  /// ## Phase C: THIS is where an uploaded brochure lands, and there is no
  /// `Tour.brochurePdfUrl`
  ///
  /// A `Tour.brochurePdfUrl` column was the obvious place to put the admin form's
  /// brochure upload, and it is the one shape that must not be used here: this
  /// column already exists, already means "the URL of this tour's brochure PDF",
  /// and is already the field `GET /api/brochures/[slug]` returns and
  /// `BrochureModal` consumes. Writing the upload to a NEW column on `Tour` would
  /// leave the public brochure endpoint answering `pdfUrl: null, status:
  /// PENDING_UPLOAD` for a tour whose brochure had in fact been uploaded — the
  /// admin sees a success state, the visitor still cannot download anything, and
  /// nothing anywhere reports the disagreement.
  ///
  /// That is precisely `memory/feedback_qg_unenforced-safety-flag.md`'s variant 4
  /// (the same real-world entity living in two models whose gates drift apart),
  /// and the fix there was the same: write to the model the readers already read.
  pdfUrl        String?
  /// Cloudinary `public_id` for `pdfUrl` — see `Tour.coverImagePublicId`.
  pdfPublicId   String?
  /// The admin's own filename at upload time, e.g. `spiti-2026-final.pdf`. Shown
  /// back in the editor so the admin can confirm the right file went in; a
  /// Cloudinary URL ends in an opaque id and is no help for that.
  pdfFileName   String?
  status        BrochureStatus @default(PENDING_UPLOAD)
  /// Incremented on every REPLACEMENT upload. The previous asset is deliberately
  /// not destroyed in Cloudinary, so an older version's URL keeps resolving even
  /// though this row no longer names it. A `BrochureVersion` table that made those
  /// older URLs browsable/restorable is still not modelled — see the note on
  /// `tourSlug` above; this counter is the honest half of it, not a claim that
  /// version history is implemented.
  version       Int            @default(1)
  fileSizeBytes Int?
  downloadCount Int            @default(0)
  uploadedAt    DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  @@index([status])
}
